## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Feed-forward predictions outperform optimization-based methods across multiple 3D tasks**: VGGT achieves 88.2 AUC@30 on CO3Dv2 and 85.3 on RealEstate10K for camera pose estimation in 0.2s, compared to MASt3R (81.8/76.4 with ~9s), DUST3R (76.7/67.7 with ~7s), and VGGSfM v2 (83.4/78.9 with ~10s), all of which require costly post-processing (Table 1). Similarly, on ETH3D point map estimation, VGGT achieves 0.709 Overall Chamfer distance versus MASt3R's 0.826, despite MASt3R using global alignment optimization (Table 3).

2. **Joint multi-task training improves per-task accuracy despite redundancy**: The ablation in Table 6 shows that removing camera supervision degrades point map Overall from 0.709 to 0.790, removing track supervision degrades it to 0.727, and removing depth degrades it to 0.834, demonstrating that training all tasks together yields the best point map accuracy even though camera+depth can deterministically produce point maps.

3. **Decomposing point maps into depth+camera at inference yields better accuracy than the dedicated head**: Table 3 shows that constructing 3D points via unprojecting predicted depth maps with predicted cameras (Overall 0.677) outperforms the direct point map head (Overall 0.709) on ETH3D, revealing that decomposing the complex task into simpler subproblems is beneficial at inference despite joint training.

### Supporting strengths

1. **Alternating-Attention design is architecturally effective**: Table 5 shows that the Alternating-Attention variant (Overall 0.709) outperforms both global-only self-attention (0.827) and cross-attention (1.061) on ETH3D point map estimation, with all variants using the same parameter count.

2. **Strong performance without ground-truth cameras on DTU**: VGGT achieves 0.382 Overall Chamfer distance without GT cameras, compared to DUST3R's 1.741 and comparable to methods that use GT cameras (e.g., GeoMVSNet at 0.295, PatchmatchNet at 0.417) (Table 2).

3. **Pretrained features transfer effectively to downstream tasks**: Replacing CoTracker2's backbone with VGGT features improves δ_avg^vis from 78.9 to 84.0 on TAP-Vid RGB-S and AJ from 49.6 to 57.2 on TAP-Vid Kinetics (Table 8), despite VGGT being trained on static scenes. For novel view synthesis, VGGT-NVS achieves competitive results (PSNR 30.41) without input camera parameters and with only 20% of LVSM's training data (Table 7).

4. **Generalization to unseen dataset**: VGGT achieves 85.3 AUC@30 on RealEstate10K, on which none of the compared methods were trained, surpassing VGGSfM v2 (78.9) and all other baselines (Table 1).

## Summary

VGGT is a large feed-forward transformer that jointly predicts camera parameters, depth maps, point maps, and point tracks from one or many images in a single forward pass, outperforming optimization-based alternatives (e.g., DUST3R, MASt3R, VGGSfM) across camera pose estimation, multi-view depth, and point map benchmarks. The most important piece of evidence is Table 1, where VGGT achieves 85.3/88.2 AUC@30 on RealEstate10K/CO3Dv2 in 0.2s—surpassing all prior methods including those requiring expensive post-processing optimization—combined with Table 6 showing that this performance stems from jointly training all 3D tasks together rather than treating them independently.

